nonutrition.DARWISH A:Darwish A is a researcher who has studied the effects of immunonutrition in surgical oncology.O'REILLY DA:O'Reilly DA is a researcher involved in studies on immunonutrition and its effects on surgical outcomes.DENTON J:Denton J is a researcher who has contributed to studies on immunonutrition in surgical patients.SHEEN AJ:Sheen AJ is a researcher involved in studies on the effects of immunonutrition in surgery.CHANG D:Chang D is a researcher who has studied the impact of immunonutrition on surgical recovery.SZYBINSKI P:Szybinski P is a researcher who has contributed to studies on immunonutrition in surgical patients.SZCZEPANEK K:Szczepanek K is a researcher involved in studies on the effects of immunonutrition in surgery.SCISLO L:Scislo L is a researcher who has studied the impact of immunonutrition on surgical recovery.WALEWSKA E:Walewska E is a researcher involved in studies on immunNUTRITIONAL SUPPORT IN ONCOLOGY:Nutritional support in oncology refers to the assessment and management of nutritional needs for cancer patients to improve their health outcomes.NUTRITION MANAGEMENT:Nutrition management involves the planning and implementation of dietary strategies to support health, particularly in patients with specific medical conditions.RISK FACTORS:Risk factors are characteristics or conditions that increase the likelihood of developing a disease or injury.NUPAC:NUPAC is a study focused on the nutritional assessment of cancer patients, highlighting the prevalence of malnutrition in this population.VSG-PG:VSG-PG (Valoraci√≥n Subjetiva Global Generada por el Paciente) is a subjective global assessment tool used to evaluate the nutritional status of patients, particularly in oncology.PROTE√ìLISIS:La prote√≥lisis es el proceso de descomposici√≥n de prote√≠nas, que puede ser exacerbado en pacientes con c√°ncer, afectando la masa muscular.ULCERACI√ìN ORAL:La ulceraci√≥n oral es una condici√≥n que puede afectar a pacientes con c√°ncer, dificultando la ingesta de alimentos y contribuyendo a la malnutrici√≥n.DENTICI√ìN INADECUADA:La dentici√≥n inadecuada puede dificultar la alimentaci√≥n en pacientes con c√°ncer, contribuyendo a la malnutrici√≥n.MOTILIDAD INTESTINAL REDUCIDA:La motilidad intestinal reducida puede ser un efecto secundario en pacientes con c√°ncer, afectando su capacidad para digerir alimentos.DOLOR NO CONTROLADO:El dolor no controlado puede afectar la capacidad de los pacientes con c√°ncer para alimentarse adecuadamente, contribuyendo a la malnutrici√≥n.EFECTOS SECUNDARIOS DE LOS TRATAMIENTOS:Los efectos secundarios de los tratamientos oncol√≥gicos pueden incluir s√≠ntomas que afectan la ingesta nutricional de los pacientes.RESERVAS CORPORALES:Las reservas corporales se refieren a la cantidad de grasa y m√∫sculo en el cuerpo, que pueden verse afectadas en pacientes con c√°ncer.TOXICIDAD DE LA QUIMIOTERAPIA:La toxicidad de la quimioterapia puede afectar la salud general de los pacientes con c√°ncer, incluyendo su estado nutricional.)<|COMPLETE|>MARTIN L:Martin L is a researcher and author who has made significant contributions to the field of medical research. He studied patients' perspectives on living with a PEG (Percutaneous Endoscopic Gastrostomy), providing valuable insights into their experiences and challenges. Additionally, Martin L contributed to a study on cancer cachexia, exploring its implications in obesity, further highlighting his commitment to understanding complex health issues.BIRDSELL L:Birdsell L is a co-author of the research on cancer cachexia, focusing on muscle depletion as a prognostic factor.MACDONALD N:Macdonald N is a researcher involved in the study of cancer cachexia and its effects on patients.REIMAN T:Reiman T is a contributor to the research discussing the impact of muscle depletion in cancer patients.CLANDININ MT:Clandinin MT is a co-author of the study on cancer cachexia and its relationship with obesity.MCCARGAR LJ:McCargar LJ is a researcher who participated in the study on cancer cachexia and its implications.ONCOLOGICAL PATIENT:An oncological patient is an individual diagnosed with cancer who is undergoing treatment for the disease. These patients often face significant challenges, including complications related to their condition and the effects of treatment. Common issues they may encounter include anorexia and nutritional deficiencies, which can arise as a result of both the cancer itself and the therapies employed to combat it.INFLAMMATION:Inflammation is a common response in cancer patients that can affect metabolism and contribute to cachexia.INMUNOTERAPIA:La inmunoterapia es un tratamiento que puede causar cansancio, n√°useas y anorexia, y a menudo se combina con quimioterapia en casos avanzados.PACIENTE NEOPL√ÅSICO:El paciente neopl√°sico es una persona diagnosticada con c√°ncer que enfrenta desaf√≠os nutricionales y de salud debido a la enfermedad y sus tratamientos.MALNUTRICI√ìN EN PACIENTES CON C√ÅNCER:La malnutrici√≥n en pacientes con c√°ncer se asocia con un pron√≥stico desfavorable y puede afectar la eficacia del tratamiento.AGENTES FARMACOL√ìGICOS:Los agentes farmacol√≥gicos son tratamientos utilizados en pacientes malnutridos para estimular el apetito y mejorar la funci√≥n intestinal.PROFESIONAL NUTRICIONAL:El profesional nutricional es un experto que proporciona asesoramiento nutricional a los pacientes para mejorar sus h√°bitos alimentarios.NUTRITION RISK SCREENING 2002:Nutrition Risk Screening 2002 (NRS-2002) is a validated tool used for nutritional screening to identify patients at risk of malnutrition.MINI NUTRITIONAL ASSESSMENT SHORT FORM REVISED:Mini Nutritional Assessment Short Form Revised is a tool used to assess the nutritional status of elderly patients.PLIEGUES CUT√ÅNEOS:La medici√≥n de los pliegues cut√°neos es un m√©todo para valorar la grasa corporal y el compartimento muscular en pacientes.DURIN Y:Las ecuaciones de Durnin y son utilizadas para estimar los distintos compartimentos corporales en la evaluaci√≥n nutricional.ALTERACIONES PSICOL√ìGICAS:Alteraciones Psicol√≥gicas refer to the psychological changes that patients may experience when undergoing enteral nutrition. These changes can include body image issues and depressive disorders, which may significantly influence the patients' food intake and overall nutritional status.SITUACIONES DE ESTR√âS:Las situaciones de estr√©s pueden afectar los niveles de prote√≠nas plasm√°ticas y el estado nutricional de los pacientes.ENTEROPAT√çAS:Las enteropat√≠as son condiciones que pueden llevar a la p√©rdida de prote√≠nas y afectar el estado nutricional de los pacientes.TRAUMATISMO:El traumatismo puede ser un factor que afecta la evaluaci√≥n nutricional y los niveles de prote√≠nas en pacientes cr√≠ticos.PROTEIN:Proteins are essential macromolecules that play a vital role in the body's structure and function, and their levels are monitored in patientsDEPLETION:Nutritional depletion refers to the state of lacking essential nutrients, which can be assessed through various parametersPUNTOS:A scoring system used to evaluate the adequacy of a patient's nutritional intakeVALOR NORMAL:Normal values for various proteins and nutrients are established to assess patient healthDESEQUILIBRIO NUTRICIONAL:Nutritional imbalance can lead to various health complications and is monitored in patients)<|COMPLETE|>WHO/ECOG SCALE:The WHO/ECOG scale is a tool used to measure physical activity levels in patients, ranging from normal activity to being bedridden.KARNOFSKY SCALE:The Karnofsky scale is a specific tool used to assess a patient's functional status and ability to carry out daily activities.MINI NUTRITIONAL ASSESSMENT (MNA):The Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA) is a tool used to evaluate the nutritional status of adults and the elderly.NUTRITIONAL COUNSELING:Nutritional counseling involves providing dietary advice to patients to help them maintain or improve their nutritional status during treatment.NUTRIENT DEFICIENCY:Nutrient deficiency occurs when the body does not get enough of the essential nutrients it needs, which can be particularly problematic for cancer patients.EFFECTS OF TREATMENT:Effects of treatment refer to the side effects and complications that arise from cancer therapies, which can impact a patient's nutritional intake and overall health.SCREENING:Screening involves assessing patients for potential nutritional issues to provide timely interventions, particularly in oncological care. It refers to the process of evaluating individuals for malnutrition risk using various assessment tools such as the Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA), Nutritional Risk Screening 2002 (NRS 2002), and the Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool (MUST). This comprehensive approach ensures that patients receive appropriate nutritional support based on their specific needs.MIRTAPAZINA:Mirtazapina is a tetracyclic antidepressant that enhances the release of norepinephrine and serotonin, improving appetite and quality of life in cancer patients.ANABOLIZANTES:Anabolizantes, also known as anabolics, are testosterone derivatives that are considered for therapeutic use, particularly in the treatment of cancer patients. Their primary purpose is to counteract muscle mass loss, which can be a significant issue for individuals undergoing cancer treatment.HIPOGEUSIA:Hipogeusia refers to a reduced ability to taste, which can impact the dietary choices of cancer patients.DISGEUSIA:Disgeusia is a condition characterized by a distorted sense of taste, often affecting cancer patients' appetite and food preferences.ESOFAGITIS:Esofagitis is inflammation of the esophagus that can cause pain and difficulty swallowing, impacting nutrition in cancer patients.SACIEDAD PRECOZ:Saciedad precoz refers to early satiety, a condition where patients feel full after consuming only a small amount of food, affecting their nutritional intake.ENOBOSARM:Enobosarm is a selective androgen receptor modulator currently in phase II studies, showing promise in increasing lean mass and strength.DRONABINOL:Dronabinol is a synthetic form of THC used in the US as an appetite stimulant and antiemetic, with psychoactive properties.GRELINA:Grelina is a hormone that has shown to increase appetite in healthy individuals and cancer patients but is not commercially available due to its short half-life.ANAMORELINA:Anamorelina is a ghrelin analog that has demonstrated improvements in appetite, body weight, and lean mass in phase III studies, though it is not yet available.PACIENTE ONCOL√ìGICO:The "PACIENTE ONCOL√ìGICO" refers to an adult cancer patient who is undergoing treatment for cancer. This individual may experience nutritional deficiencies or malnutrition as a result of the disease and its associated treatments. Due to these challenges, the oncological patient often requires nutritional support to help maintain their health and well-being throughout the treatment process.C√ÅNCER PULMONAR:El c√°ncer pulmonar es un tipo de c√°ncer que tambi√©n puede aumentar el gasto energ√©tico en reposo de los pacientes.DIETA TRADICIONAL:La dieta tradicional es la alimentaci√≥n habitual que se modifica para mejorar la nutrici√≥n de los pacientes oncol√≥gicos.SUPLEMENTOS DE ISOFLAVONAS:Los suplementos que contienen isoflavonas no se aconsejan para pacientes oncol√≥gicos debido a su contenido elevado en comparaci√≥n con la dieta.DERIVADOS DE SOJA:Los derivados de soja son productos que no se pueden recomendar actualmente para pacientes oncol√≥gicos debido a la falta de informaci√≥n sobre sus interacciones.LESLER M:Lesler M is an author of the work titled 'Oncology Nutrition for Clinical Practice' published in 2013, which discusses nutritional needs for cancer patients.ONCOLOGY NUTRITION FOR CLINICAL PRACTICE:A publication that provides guidelines and recommendations for nutritional support in oncology patients.HEMATOPOIETIC TRANSPLANT:A medical procedure that may require specific nutritional support as indicated in the guidelines.PROTEIN NEEDS:Protein needs refer to the specific dietary requirements for protein intake, which can vary significantly for patients, especially those with cancer or undergoing treatment.ALIMENTOS DE ALTO CONTENIDO CAL√ìRICO:Los alimentos de alto contenido cal√≥rico son aquellos que proporcionan una gran cantidad de energ√≠a y son recomendados para pacientes con desnutrici√≥n.DIETA ENRIQUECIDA:La dieta enriquecida es un tipo de alimentaci√≥n que se utiliza para aumentar la ingesta cal√≥rica y proteica de los pacientes oncol√≥gicos.H√ÅBITOS ALIMENTICIOS:Los h√°bitos alimenticios son las costumbres y pr√°cticas relacionadas con la alimentaci√≥n, que deben ser consideradas en el consejo nutricional para pacientes oncol√≥gicos.ASOCIACI√ìN EUROPEA DE NUTRICI√ìN ENTERAL Y PARENTERAL:The European Association of Enteral and Parenteral Nutrition (ESPEN) recommends preoperative enteral nutrition for patients undergoing major abdominal surgery.TUMOR NO OPERABLE:An inoperable tumor is a type of cancer that cannot be surgically removed, impacting nutritional intake.FORMULAS ENRIQUECIDAS:Enriched formulas are specialized nutritional products designed to provide additional nutrients for patients undergoing surgery.HIPOREXIA:Hyporexia is a condition characterized by a reduced appetite, often seen in cancer patients.SENSACI√ìN DE SACIEDAD PRECOZ:Early satiety is a feeling of fullness that occurs after eating only a small amount of food, common in cancer patients.NUTRITIONAL PARENTERAL:Nutritional parenteral is a method of delivering nutrients directly into the bloodstream, bypassing the gastrointestinal tract, often used for patients unable to consume food orally.NUTRITIONAL ENTERAL:COCHRANE LIBRARY:The Cochrane Library is a collection of databases that provide systematic reviews of healthcare interventions, including studies on nutritional supplements and their effects on patients.PROTOCOLO ERAS:Enhanced Recovery After Surgery protocol aimed at improving recovery outcomes for surgical patients, particularly in oncology.ADENOCARCINOMAS INTRAABDOMINALES:Advanced intra-abdominal adenocarcinomas that may necessitate specialized nutritional formulas.CANCER PATIENT:Cancer patients are individuals diagnosed with cancer who may require nutritional support due to the effects of the disease and its treatment.NUTRITIONAL VALUATION:Nutritional valuation is the assessment of a patient's nutritional status to determine the appropriate support needed, particularly in oncology patients.IMMUNOCOMPROMISED PATIENT:Immunocompromised patients are individuals whose immune systems are weakened, making them more susceptible to infections, particularly relevant in the context of nutritional support.CHRISTOU NV:Christou NV is an organization involved in medical research, particularly in estimating mortality risk in preoperative patients.TELLADO RODRIGUEZ J:Tellado Rodr√≠guez J is a researcher contributing to studies on mortality risk in preoperative patients.CHARTRAND L:Chartrand L is a researcher involved in the study of mortality risk in preoperative patients.JOURNAL OF CLINICAL ONCOLOGY:Journal of Clinical Oncology is a journal that published studies on dietary counseling and its impact on cancer patient outcomes.HEAD AND NECK:Head and Neck is a journal that published studies on the impact of nutrition on outcomes in head and neck cancer patients.CURRENT ONCOLOGY:Current Oncology is a journal that published evaluations of nutrition-rehabilitation programs for advanced cancer patients.AMERICAN JOURNAL OF CLINICAL ONCOLOGY:American Journal of Clinical Oncology published toxicity and response criteria for cancer treatment.NUTRITIONAL SUPPORT OF CANCER PATIENTS:Nutritional support of cancer patients is a critical area of research addressing the needs and challenges faced by cancer patients.CANDELA C. G.:Candela C. G. is a researcher involved in algorithms for nutritional assessment and treatment in adult cancer patients.TUTEN MB:RAVASCO P:Ravasco P is a researcher who has focused on the impact of dietary counseling on patient outcomes specifically for cancer patients undergoing radiotherapy.GAGNON B:OKEN MM:YATES JW:LITTLE JM:Little JM is a researcher who co-authored a study comparing serum transferrin and prealbumin as nutritional parameters.GUEST PK:Guest PK is a researcher who co-authored a study comparing serum transferrin and prealbumin as nutritional parameters.MONTEIRO-GRILLO I:Monteiro-Grillo I is a researcher who studied the impact of nutrition on outcomes in head and neck cancer patients.VIDAL PM:Vidal PM is a researcher who studied the impact of nutrition on outcomes in head and neck cancer patients.CAMILO ME:Camilo ME is a researcher who studied the impact of nutrition on outcomes in head and neck cancer patients.DEL FABBRO E:Del Fabbro E is a researcher who studied clinical outcomes and contributors to weight loss in a cancer cachexia clinic.HUI D:Hui D is a researcher who contributed to the study on clinical outcomes in a cancer cachexia clinic.DALAL S:Dalal S is a researcher who contributed to the study on clinical outcomes in a cancer cachexia clinic.DEV R:Dev R is a researcher who contributed to the study on clinical outcomes in a cancer cachexia clinic.NOORUDDIN ZI:Nooruddin ZI is a researcher who contributed to the study on clinical outcomes in a cancer cachexia clinic.BRUERA E:Bruera E is a researcher who contributed to the study on clinical outcomes in a cancer cachexia clinic.NITENBERG G:Nitenberg G is a researcher who has made significant contributions to the study of nutritional support for cancer patients, specifically focusing on enteral nutritional support. Through his work, he has addressed various issues related to nutritional support in this patient population, highlighting the importance of adequate nutrition in the management of cancer.RAYNARD B:Raynard B is a researcher who studied nutritional support issues for cancer patients.BOZZETTI V:Bozzetti V is a researcher who studied the adequacy of intravenous amino acid administration in cancer patients.ARINDS J:Arends J is a researcher who contributed to the ESPEN Guidelines on Enteral Nutrition.COSS CC:Coss CC is a researcher who studied cancer cachexia therapy.BOHLE CE:Bohl CE is a researcher who studied cancer cachexia therapy.DALTON JT:Dalton JT is a researcher who studied cancer cachexia therapy.BJELEKOVIC G:Bjelakovic G is a researcher who conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis on antioxidant supplements for cancer prevention.NORMAN HA:Norman HA is a researcher who studied the role of dietary supplements during cancer therapy.FELDMAN E:Feldman E is a researcher who studied the role of dietary supplements during cancer therapy.HEBER D:Heber D is a researcher who studied the role of dietary supplements during cancer therapy.PICCIANO MF:Picciano MF is a researcher who studied the role of dietary supplements during cancer therapy.FLETCHER JP:J NUTRITION:HOSPITAL UNIVERSITARIO 12 DE OCTUBRE:Hospital Universitario 12 de Octubre is a medical institution located in Madrid, Spain. It specializes in pediatrics, endocrinology, and clinical nutrition, providing a comprehensive range of healthcare services in these fields.M.¬™ √ÅNGELES VALERO ZANUY:M.¬™ √Ångeles Valero Zanuy is a medical professional specializing in nutrition at Hospital Universitario 12 de Octubre.TRASPLANTE DE C√âLULAS HEMATOL√ìGICAS:The "TRASPLANTE DE C√âLULAS HEMATOL√ìGICAS" is a medical procedure that involves the transfer of hematopoietic stem cells to treat a variety of diseases, including certain types of cancer, blood disorders, and hematological tumors. This treatment is considered potentially curative for various hematological diseases and some solid tumors. The procedure typically requires the administration of high doses of chemotherapy followed by the infusion of hematopoietic cells. Additionally, the context of this transplantation includes discussions on the importance of nutritional support for patients undergoing the procedure, highlighting the comprehensive care needed during treatment.ENFERMEDAD DE INJERTO CONTRA HU√âSPED:Graft-versus-host disease (GVHD), known in Spanish as "Enfermedad de Injerto Contra Hu√©sped" (EICH), is a serious complication that can arise following hematopoietic cell transplantation. This condition occurs when the transplanted cells, or graft, initiate an immune response against the recipient's body, leading to significant morbidity and mortality. GVHD is characterized by the transplanted cells attacking the recipient's tissues, which can result in various clinical manifestations. The disease highlights the complexities and risks associated with hematopoietic cell transplants, underscoring the importance of monitoring and managing potential complications in transplant recipients.ENFERMEDAD VENOCLOSIVA:La enfermedad venoclusiva es una complicaci√≥n que puede surgir tras un trasplante de c√©lulas hematol√≥gicas, caracterizada por la obstrucci√≥n de las venas del h√≠gado.APLASIA:Aplasia is a medical condition characterized by the absence or significant reduction of normal cell production in the bone marrow. This condition often arises as a complication following a hematopoietic stem cell transplant. In Spanish, it is described as "una condici√≥n en la que hay una disminuci√≥n o ausencia de c√©lulas en la m√©dula √≥sea," highlighting its impact on the bone marrow's ability to produce essential blood cells. Aplasia can lead to various health issues due to the insufficient production of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, which are crucial for overall bodily functions.CITOST√ÅTICOS:Los citost√°ticos son medicamentos utilizados en quimioterapia para detener el crecimiento de c√©lulas cancerosas y son administrados durante el trasplante de c√©lulas hematol√≥gicas.C√âLULAS HEMATOL√ìGICAS:C√âLULAS HEMATOL√ìGICAS son las c√©lulas de la sangre que desempe√±an un papel crucial en los trasplantes, utilizados para tratar diversas enfermedades hematol√≥gicas.ALOG√âNICO:Alog√©nico refers to a type of bone marrow transplantation in which the donor is a different individual, as opposed to an autologous transplant where the donor and recipient are the same person. This procedure often necessitates additional nutritional support for the recipient. The alog√©nico transplant involves the use of compatible donor cells to treat both malignant and non-malignant diseases.AUT√ìLOGO:AUT√ìLOGO refers to a type of bone marrow transplantation in which the donor is the patient themselves. This procedure utilizes the patient's own hematological cells, thereby avoiding issues related to compatibility.MEDULA √ìSEA:La m√©dula √≥sea es el tejido donde se producen las c√©lulas sangu√≠neas y de donde se obtienen c√©lulas para trasplantes.SANGRE PERIF√âRICA:La sangre perif√©rica es la sangre que circula en el cuerpo y de la cual se pueden obtener c√©lulas hematol√≥gicas mediante af√©resis.CORD√ìN UMBILICAL:El cord√≥n umbilical es una fuente de c√©lulas madre hematopoy√©ticas que se utilizan en trasplantes, especialmente en ni√±os.H√çGADO FETAL:El h√≠gado fetal es un √≥rgano de donde se pueden obtener c√©lulas hematopoy√©ticas en casos espec√≠ficos de trasplante.LEUCEMIA:La leucemia es un tipo de c√°ncer que afecta a las c√©lulas sangu√≠neas y que puede ser tratada mediante trasplante de c√©lulas hematol√≥gicas.DREPANOCITOSIS:La drepanocitosis es una enfermedad gen√©tica de la sangre que puede ser tratada con trasplante de c√©lulas hematopoy√©ticas.ERITROBLASTOPENIA DE BLACKFAN-DIAMOND<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7NEUTROPENIA DE KOSTMANN<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7S√çNDROME DE WISKOTT-ALDRICH<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7OSTEOPETROSIS JUVENIL<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7TESAURISMOSIS<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7ENFERMEDAD GRANULOMATOSA CR√ìNICA<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7LEUCEMIA AGUDA<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:9LEUCEMIA MIELOIDE CR√ìNICA<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:9LEUCEMIA LINF√ÅTICA CR√ìNICA<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:9LINFOMA NO-HODGKIN<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:9LINFOMA DE HODGKIN<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:9MIELOMA M√öLTIPLE<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:9HISTIOCITOSIS<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7AMILOIDOSIS<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7S√çNDROMES MIELODISPL√ÅSICOS<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7TUMORES S√ìLIDOS<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7ENFERMEDADES AUTOINMUNES<|DIFF_MARKER|>EVENT:7)<|COMPLETE|>CD4+ CELLS:A type of immune cell that plays a crucial role in the immune response, often monitored in patients post-transplant.NK CELLS:Natural Killer cells that are part of the immune system and can be affected during the post-transplant recovery phase.CD8+ CELLS:A type of T cell that is important for the immune response, particularly in recognizing and destroying infected or cancerous cells.TRANSPLANT CANDIDATE:A patient who is being considered for a hematopoietic stem cell transplant, often undergoing various preparatory treatments.SOLID TUMORS:A type of cancer that forms in solid organs or tissues, which may also be treated with hematopoietic stem cell transplants.IMMUNE RECONSTITUTION:The process by which the immune system recovers after a hematopoietic stem cell transplant, involving the re-establishment of immune cell populations.HEMATOLOGICAL MALIGNANCIES:CELULAS HEMATOPOYETICAS:C√©lulas hematopoy√©ticas son c√©lulas madre que pueden ser trasplantadas para tratar diversas enfermedades, incluyendo c√°nceres hematol√≥gicos.ONCOLOGY SCREENING TOOL:El Oncology Screening Tool (OST) es una herramienta dise√±ada espec√≠ficamente para evaluar el estado nutricional de pacientes oncol√≥gicos, incluyendo aquellos que se someten a trasplantes de c√©lulas hematol√≥gicas.PERSONAL SANITARIO:El "PERSONAL SANITARIO" se refiere a los profesionales de la salud que operan dentro del Sistema Nacional de Salud. Este grupo incluye m√©dicos y nutricionistas que desempe√±an un papel crucial en la evaluaci√≥n y gesti√≥n del estado nutricional de los pacientes, tanto antes como despu√©s del trasplante de c√©lulas hematopoy√©ticas. Su labor es fundamental para asegurar que los pacientes reciban la atenci√≥n adecuada y optimicen su recuperaci√≥n durante este proceso cr√≠tico.ENFERMEDADES ADQUIRIDAS:Las enfermedades adquiridas son condiciones que se desarrollan despu√©s del nacimiento y pueden influir en la necesidad de trasplante de c√©lulas hematopoy√©ticas.DESNUTRICION:La desnutrici√≥n es una condici√≥n que puede afectar a los pacientes antes y despu√©s de un trasplante, y es crucial monitorizarla para asegurar una recuperaci√≥n adecuada.ENFERMEDADES CONGENITAS:ENFERMEDAD DE INJERTO CONTRA HU√âSPED (EICH):La enfermedad de injerto contra hu√©sped es una complicaci√≥n grave del trasplante de c√©lulas hematopoy√©ticas, donde las c√©lulas inmunitarias del donante atacan los tejidos del receptor.DATOS ANTROPOM√âTRICOS:Los datos antropom√©tricos son medidas del cuerpo humano, como peso y altura, que se utilizan para evaluar el estado nutricional de los pacientes, especialmente en aquellos que han recibido un trasplante de c√©lulas hematol√≥gicas.EICH AGUDA:La EICH aguda se presenta en los primeros d√≠as postrasplante, afectando principalmente a la piel, h√≠gado e intestino, y requiere tratamiento inmediato con corticoides y otros f√°rmacos.ENFERMEDAD VENOCLUSIVA HEP√ÅTICA:La enfermedad venoclusiva hep√°tica es un conjunto de s√≠ntomas y signos hep√°ticos que afecta a pacientes que han recibido un trasplante, con una incidencia del 20%. Esta condici√≥n puede surgir tras el trasplante y tiene un impacto significativo en la funci√≥n del h√≠gado.ALOANT√çGENOS:Los aloant√≠genos son ant√≠genos que son diferentes entre individuos de la misma especie, y pueden provocar una respuesta inmune en el receptor de un injerto.C√âLULAS HEMATOPI√âTICAS:C√©lulas hematopoy√©ticas son c√©lulas madre que se desarrollan en la m√©dula √≥sea y son responsables de la producci√≥n de todas las c√©lulas sangu√≠neas.DIARREA SECRETORA:La diarrea secretora es una condici√≥n que puede causar p√©rdidas importantes de electrolitos en pacientes trasplantados.TRANSPLANTE HEMATOL√ìGICO:El trasplante hematol√≥gico es un procedimiento m√©dico en el que se transfieren c√©lulas madre hematopoy√©ticas a un paciente para tratar diversas enfermedades, incluyendo c√°ncer y trastornos de la sangre.MUSCARITOLI ET AL:Muscaritoli et al son investigadores que han estudiado el impacto de la administraci√≥n de l√≠pidos en la incidencia de EICH en trasplantes alog√©nicos.CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL:The Centers for Disease Control (CDC) is a national public health institute in the United States that plays a crucial role in providing guidelines on health practices and disease prevention. The CDC offers comprehensive recommendations aimed at promoting health and preventing diseases, which include specific dietary guidelines for patients undergoing hematopoietic cell transplants. As a key agency in public health, the CDC is dedicated to improving health outcomes and ensuring the well-being of the population through its evidence-based directives.ALIMENTOS COMERCIALMENTE EST√âRILES:Commercially sterile foods are those with extremely low levels of microorganisms, prepared in controlled environments to ensure safety for patients.ALIMENTOS MUY LIMPIOS:Very clean foods are fresh foods with lower microorganism content than regular hospital diets, prepared under strict hygiene conditions.ACOR:ACOR is an organization that provides information related to leukemia and patient care, including dietary recommendations for patients undergoing treatment.CDC:The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) is a national public health institute in the United States that plays a crucial role in providing health guidelines and standards. It is responsible for disseminating information related to health and safety, including specific growth patterns for children. The CDC's mission encompasses a wide range of public health initiatives aimed at improving the overall health of the population.FSIS:The Food Safety and Inspection Service (FSIS) is an agency of the United States Department of Agriculture responsible for ensuring the safety of the nation's commercial supply of meat, poultry, and egg products.FOOD SAFETY:Food safety refers to the practices and guidelines that ensure food is safe to eat, particularly important for immunocompromised patients.HEMATOLOGICAL CELL TRANSPLANT:Hematological cell transplant is a medical procedure used to treat certain types of cancer and blood disorders, requiring specific dietary precautions.ANTINEOPLASTIC TREATMENT:Antineoplastic treatment refers to therapies used to treat cancer, which can impact a patient's nutritional status and dietary needs.MICROORGANISMS:Microorganisms are tiny living organisms that can be beneficial or harmful, particularly relevant in the context of food safety for immunocompromised patients.STERILIZATION:Sterilization is a process used to eliminate all forms of life, including microorganisms, from utensils and equipment used in healthcare settings.UTENSILS:Utensils refer to tools used for eating and serving food, which must be properly sanitized for patients with weakened immune systems.LEUKEMIA:SOCIEDAD EUROPEA DE NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL Y ENTERAL:ESPEN is an organization that offers clinical guidelines for artificial nutritional support in patients undergoing hematopoietic cell transplantation.PANCITOPENIA:Pancitopenia is a medical condition characterized by a reduction in red and white blood cells and platelets, often seen in patients undergoing chemotherapy.DIFICULTADES DE TOLERANCIA:Dificultades de tolerancia refers to the challenges patients face when adapting to nutritional interventions, such as enteral or parenteral nutrition.BONE MARROW TRANSPLANT:Bone Marrow Transplant refers to both a medical procedure and a scholarly journal. As a medical procedure, it involves the replacement or supplementation of bone marrow, often necessitating nutritional support due to potential complications. This procedure typically entails the transplantation of blood stem cells to treat a variety of conditions, including cancers and blood disorders. Additionally, Bone Marrow Transplant is a journal dedicated to publishing research related to bone marrow transplantation and associated studies, contributing to the academic and clinical understanding of this important medical field.HEMATOLOGICAL PATIENT:Patients suffering from blood-related diseases who may require nutritional support during treatment, especially after bone marrow transplants.CATHETER:A catheter is a thin tube inserted into the body to deliver or remove fluids, commonly utilized in medical settings. It serves as a medical device for administering nutritional support, although it carries risks of infection if not managed properly.PACIENTES CON TRASPLANTE DE C√âLULAS HEMATOL√ìGICAS:Patients undergoing hematological cell transplants who may receive glutamine supplementation.ESTUDIOS EN RATAS:Studies conducted on rats demonstrate the benefits of glutamine supplementation in catabolic states.ADMINISTRACI√ìN INTRAVENOSA DE AMINO√ÅCIDOS:Intravenous administration of amino acids, including glutamine, is used to prevent loss from muscle during critical illness.ALANINA:AQUINO:Aquino es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en ni√±os sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.BLIJLEVENS:Blijlevens es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en adultos sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.CANOVAS:Canovas es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en pacientes sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.COGHLIN DICKSON:Coghlin Dickson es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en adultos sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.DA GAMA TORRES:Da Gama Torres es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en adultos sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.G√ìMEZ-CANDELA:G√≥mez-Candela es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en adultos sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.JEBB:Jebb es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en pacientes sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.MASSZI:Masszi es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en adultos sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.PICARDI:Picardi es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en pacientes sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.PICCIRILLO:Piccirillo es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en adultos sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.PYTLIK:Pytlik es un autor de un estudio que investiga la administraci√≥n de glutamina en adultos sometidos a trasplante hematol√≥gico.ANDERSON:TUMORES S√ìLIDOS:Tumores s√≥lidos are solid tumors that can lead to complications and may require nutritional support during treatment.CITOKINAS INFLAMATORIAS:Cytokines are inflammatory substances that can be elevated in patients undergoing treatment and may contribute to complications.ENFERMEDAD VENOCULOSIVA HEP√ÅTICA:Hepatic veno-occlusive disease is a complication that can occur after transplantation, impacting the patient's health.TUMORES HEMATOL√ìGICOS:HOSPITALIZED OBESE PATIENTS:Hospitalized obese patients are individuals with obesity who are receiving medical care in a hospital setting, often facing unique nutritional challenges.HEMATOPOIETIC CELL TRANSPLANTATION:Hematopoietic cell transplantation is a medical procedure utilized to treat a range of diseases, particularly various cancers and blood disorders. This procedure involves the infusion of blood-forming stem cells, which serve to replace damaged or diseased bone marrow with healthy cells. By restoring the bone marrow's function, hematopoietic cell transplantation aims to improve the patient's overall health and combat the effects of the underlying conditions.CANCER MANAGEMENT:Cancer management refers to the comprehensive approach to treating cancer, including surgery, chemotherapy, radiation, and supportive care.NUTRITIONAL ISSUES:Nutritional issues in cancer management involve the challenges and considerations of providing adequate nutrition to cancer patients during treatment.NOSOCOMIAL INFECTION:Nosocomial infection refers to infections acquired in a hospital setting, which can pose significant risks to patients, especially those undergoing treatments like bone marrow transplantation.AUTLOGOUS BONE MARROW TRANSPLANTATION:Autologous bone marrow transplantation is a procedure where a patient's own bone marrow is used for transplantation, often after high-dose chemotherapy.ALLOGENIC BONE MARROW TRANSPLANTATION:Allogenic bone marrow transplantation involves using bone marrow from a donor to treat various blood disorders, including cancers.MARROW TRANSPLANT GROUP:The Marrow Transplant Group is an organization focused on the research and treatment of patients undergoing bone marrow transplants.INFECTIOUS DISEASES SOCIETY OF AMERICA:The Infectious Diseases Society of America is a professional organization dedicated to the study and treatment of infectious diseases.SOCIETY FOR HEALTHCARE EPIDEMIOLOGY OF AMERICA:The Society for Healthcare Epidemiology of America is an organization that focuses on the prevention of healthcare-associated infections and the promotion of patient safety.ASSOCIATION OF MEDICAL MICROBIOLOGY AND INFECTIOUS DISEASE CANADA:This association represents professionals in medical microbiology and infectious diseases in Canada, promoting research and education in the field.CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL AND PREVENTION:The CDC is a national public health institute in the United States, focused on disease prevention and control, including guidelines for various medical procedures.AMERICAN SOCIETY OF CLINICAL ONCOLOGY:The American Society of Clinical Oncology is a professional organization for physicians and oncology professionals dedicated to advancing cancer care and research.SCHMITZ N:Schmitz N is a researcher who has contributed to the field of hematopoietic cell transplantation.NIETHAMMER D:Niethammer D is a research